${localWorkspaceFolderBasename}: the egress proxy for proxy-client sandboxes (agent-mitm-proxy).
It refuses every host that isn't in its allowlist, and logs each refusal.

Allowing a host
  sandbox exec ${localWorkspaceFolderBasename} approve
        go through the hosts refused in the last 5 minutes; for each, y adds it,
        Enter or n skips, q stops. --since 1h looks further back.
  sandbox exec ${localWorkspaceFolderBasename} approve --list
        only list them
  Or edit the allowlist directly, one host:port per line, # for comments:
        ${localWorkspaceFolder}/allowlist.txt
  The proxy applies changes without a restart.

  approve never offers IP addresses, or names that resolve to private
  addresses (such as cloud metadata): the proxy refuses those even when listed.
  It asks only when you run it at a terminal. Client sandboxes can't run it:
  they reach the proxy only on port 8899.

Logs, inside (sandbox exec ${localWorkspaceFolderBasename} tail -f ~/proxy-data/...)
  ~/proxy-data/denied.jsonl            refused connections
  ~/proxy-data/proxy.log               the proxy's own log, with an access line per request
  ~/proxy-data/decrypted-traffic.jsonl request and response bodies (JSON as JSON), with the
                                       credentials and identity of Claude Code's endpoints
                                       redacted; what the agent sends is not: sensitive

Credential vault (option credentialVault=1 when the proxy was made)
  Claude Code's OAuth tokens stay in the proxy; clients sign in as usual but hold
  only placeholders (agent-mitm-proxy-oat-..., -ort-...), swapped for the real
  tokens on the way to Anthropic.
  A new proxy-client fetches its credentials file (the placeholders) from the
  proxy, so only the first one has to sign in.
  ~/proxy-data/credential-vault.json   the real tokens: private
  ~/proxy-data/vault.jsonl             sign-ins, refreshes, swaps, deliveries; never token values

Clients (option clientAuth=1 when the proxy was made)
  Each client needs a name and token of its own; a proxy-client made with
  clientAuth=1 enrols itself when it first starts. Others get 407.
  sandbox exec ${localWorkspaceFolderBasename} vault clients          the enrolled names
  sandbox exec ${localWorkspaceFolderBasename} vault unenroll NAME    end NAME's token, at once
  sandbox exec ${localWorkspaceFolderBasename} vault grant NAME       give NAME the vault's sign-in
  sandbox exec ${localWorkspaceFolderBasename} vault entries          the sign-ins, without tokens
  ~/proxy-data/clients.json            token hashes: private
  ~/proxy-data/clients.jsonl           refused connections; never token values

Every sandbox also has: sandbox enter, sandbox inspect, sandbox exec NAME COMMAND.
